Function and bi-function.

A lambda needs a functional-interface type to be. You could declare one every time, but the standard library already ships the common shapes, in a package called java-util-function — a small vocabulary of ready-made interfaces that almost every lambda you write will target. And the most fundamental is function: something that takes one value and returns another.

Function of T and R maps a T to an R. Its single method is apply. So a function from string to Integer, s, arrow, s-length, applied to "hello", gives five. A function from Integer to Integer, n, arrow, n times n, applied to six, gives thirty-six. So function of string, Integer, is "give me a string, I'll give you an Integer". It's the shape behind stream dot map — a mapping is a function. And the two type parameters are input, then output: function of T and R means T in, R out.

When the transformation needs two inputs, you reach for bi-function, of T, U, and R — it takes a T and a U, and returns an R. So a bi-function of Integer, Integer, Integer, a, b, arrow, a plus b, applied to two and three, gives five. Or a bi-function of string, Integer, string, s, n, arrow, s repeated n times, applied to "ab" and three, gives "ababab". Note that Java stops at two inputs — there's no tri-function. If you genuinely need three, pass a record, or curry — a function returning a function, which is section nine.

Very often, the input and output are the same type. And rather than write function of Integer, Integer, you use the operator aliases, which are exactly that, with the types collapsed. Unary-operator of T is a function from T to T — unary-operator of string, assigned String double-colon trim. And binary-operator of T is a bi-function of T, T, T — binary-operator of Integer, assigned Integer double-colon sum. List dot replace-all takes a unary-operator, and stream dot reduce takes a binary-operator — reduction, for instance, folds a stream with a binary-operator.

There's one more family: primitive specializations, for avoiding boxing. Because a function of Integer, Integer boxes every int into an Integer, the package also ships primitive variants that work on raw int, long, and double — int-function, to-int-function, int-unary-operator, and so on. You don't need to memorise them; just know they exist, so a hot numeric pipeline needn't box. And the naming is systematic: int-function takes an int, to-int-function returns an int, and int-unary-operator is int to int.

So the takeaway. Function and bi-function are the "compute a result" shapes — one or two inputs, one output — and their same-type aliases, unary-operator and binary-operator, cover the common case where nothing changes type. Reach for these standard interfaces, instead of declaring your own; your lambda just supplies apply, and library methods like map, reduce, and replace-all are typed in terms of them. The next section covers the other half of the vocabulary — the interfaces that test, with predicate; consume, with consumer; and produce, with supplier.
